\section{One row is not one line of the file, for \code{_ms1.feature}}{


An \code{_ms1.feature} row is a deconvolved \strong{neutral mass spanning a charge range}, and mzLib
expands it into one single-charge feature per charge in that range. A hundred-feature file can
read as a thousand rows. \strong{Dinosaur is one-for-one.} Either way \code{\link{readers_read_records}} gives
you the file's own rows.

\code{intensity} is the \strong{apex} intensity, not the sum over the feature. Both formats carry a summed
intensity column too, and \code{\link{readers_read_records}} has it.

}
\section{The retention-time unit is genuinely unknown for \code{_ms1.feature}}{


TopFD wrote seconds through v1.6.2 and minutes from v1.7.0 - *within the same file type*, with
nothing in the file to say which. mzLib normalises neither, and its own deconvolution code
resorts to a heuristic (divide by 60 if the largest end time exceeds 500). This package will not
launder a guess into a stated fact, so \code{\link{readers_retention_time_in_minutes}} raises rather than
converting. Dinosaur reports minutes and converts without complaint.

}
